# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages and Netlify.
#
# Both providers read a file called `_headers` from the publish directory and use
# the same rule syntax, so this one file configures either host. Cloudflare does
# add `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` by default; it is repeated here so the
# file is also correct on a host that does not.
#
# Comments are kept outside the rule blocks: an indented line is a header to
# these parsers, so a `#` line inside a block risks being read as a malformed
# header rather than as a comment.
#
# Verified against provider documentation in September 2026. Cloudflare's own
# guidance is to avoid adding custom caching at all, because its CDN already
# revalidates with ETags and aggressive rules can pin a stale asset after a
# deploy. The rules below are limited to the cases that actually matter for an
# offline-first PWA: the shell and the worker must never be held in the HTTP
# cache, fingerprinted assets can be cached forever, and the OCR payloads must
# stay revalidatable because their filenames are stable across versions.

# CardVault holds contacts in IndexedDB and never sends them anywhere. No page
# should be able to leak a referring URL, be framed, or prompt for a sensor the
# app does not use: the camera is reached through <input type="file" capture>,
# not getUserMedia, so denying it costs nothing today.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()

# The service worker decides when a new version is applied, and the app keeps
# skipWaiting off on purpose so an in-progress scan is never interrupted. That
# only works if the browser can always see the current sw.js.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Same reasoning for the app shell: a new deploy has to be visible immediately,
# otherwise the shell and the worker disagree about which assets exist.
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Vite fingerprints these filenames, so the contents at a given URL never change.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The OCR language data and core are large but their filenames are stable, so
# they must stay revalidatable: a new OCR version can replace a file in place.
/ocr/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate